Top 10 Real-Time Kubernetes Troubleshooting Commands

1. 🔍 Check Pod Status

Command: kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
📌 Use when: You want to see the status of all pods in a namespace.
✅ Example: kubectl get pods -n dev

2. 📄 Describe a Pod

Command: kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>
📌 Use when: You want to know detailed info like events, restarts, volumes.
✅ Example: kubectl describe pod nginx-74f94f8b4c-abc12 -n dev

3. 🐛 View Pod Logs

Command: kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>
📌 Use when: You're debugging app logs inside the pod.
✅ Example: kubectl logs myapp-pod-1234 -n dev
💡 For multi-container pods: kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name> -n <namespace>

4. 🧪 Check Events in Namespace

Command: kubectl get events -n <namespace> --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp
📌 Use when: You want recent event history (crash loops, OOMs, etc.)
✅ Example: kubectl get events -n dev --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp

5. 🧰 Exec Into a Pod

Command: kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -n <namespace> -- /bin/bash
📌 Use when: You want to troubleshoot inside the container (e.g., curl, ping)
✅ Example: kubectl exec -it myapp-pod -n dev -- /bin/bash

6. 🔄 Restart a Deployment

Command: kubectl rollout restart deployment <deployment-name> -n <namespace>
📌 Use when: You need to restart a deployment (e.g., config change)
✅ Example: kubectl rollout restart deployment my-deployment -n dev

7. 🧯 Check CrashLoopBackOff or OOMKill

Command: kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>
📌 Use when: A pod keeps crashing or restarting — check under "Events"
✅ Tip: Look for OOMKilled, CrashLoopBackOff, or Back-off restarting failed container.

8. 📡 Check Node Status

Command: kubectl get nodes
📌 Use when: You suspect a node issue (NotReady, disk pressure, etc.)
✅ Example: kubectl get nodes

9. 🚦 Check Services and Endpoints

Command: kubectl get svc -n <namespace> && kubectl get endpoints -n <namespace>
📌 Use when: You're debugging service connectivity or DNS resolution
✅ Example: kubectl get svc -n dev && kubectl get endpoints -n dev

10. 🌐 Check Ingress and DNS

Command: kubectl get ingress -n <namespace> && kubectl describe ingress <ingress-name>
📌 Use when: You're troubleshooting web access issues (HTTP 404/502)
✅ Example: kubectl describe ingress my-ingress -n dev
